  <h2>What sits on top of the as-builts</h2>

  <h3>The design</h3>

  <p>An architect or designer does not start with a blank page. They start with a drawing of your existing building and draw the changes on top of it. If that starting drawing is wrong, the design is wrong too, even if it looks perfect on screen.</p>

  <h3>The permit</h3>

  <p>Most cities want to see the existing building and the proposed changes side by side. The "existing" drawings in a permit set are as-builts. If they do not match the real building, the plans can be sent back for corrections, and that costs weeks.</p>

  <h3>The price</h3>

  <p>Contractors price a job from the drawings: how many feet of wall, how many square feet of floor, how many cabinets. Wrong sizes mean a wrong price. That usually shows up later as a change order, which is an extra bill in the middle of the job.</p>

  <h3>The construction</h3>

  <p>Cabinets, countertops, doors, and windows are ordered to exact sizes. Many of them cannot be returned. They are ordered from the drawings, so the drawings have to be right.</p>

  <h2>What happens when the starting point is wrong</h2>

  <p>Here is a simple example. An old plan says a kitchen wall is 12 feet long. The cabinets are ordered to fill 12 feet. On install day, the wall turns out to be 11 feet 7 inches. The last cabinet does not fit.</p>

